{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And Lemech took for himself.</b> There was no need for Scripture to state all this — only to teach us the outcome, that the Holy One, blessed be He, fulfilled His promise that He said: ״״שִׁבְעָתַיִם יֻקַּם קָיִן״ — Lemech arose after he had fathered children and produced the seventh generation, and killed Cain. This is what he said: ״כִּי אִישׁ הָרַגְתִּי לְפִצְעִי״ and so forth." "<b>Two wives.</b> Such was the way of the generation of the Flood: one for procreation and propagation, and one for intercourse. This one who was for intercourse — he would give her a cup of roots (a sterility potion) so that she would be sterilized, and he would adorn her like a bride and feed her delicacies. And her companion was rebuked and mourned like a widow. And this is what Iyov explained: ״רֹעֶה עֲקָרָה לֹא תֵלֵד וְאַלְמָנָה לֹא יְיֵטִיב״ (Job 24) — as expounded in the aggadah of Massekhet Chelek (and in Bereshit Rabbah)." "<b>Adah.</b> She is the one for procreation and propagation — so named because she is removed from him and turned away from him. עָדָה is the Targum of סוּרָה." "<b>Tzillah.</b> She is the one for intercourse — so named because she sits constantly in his shadow. These are words of aggadah in Bereshit Rabbah."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:57:36.743272959Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}